{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786531061,"nonce":"SPd21gJv3QYnuSDXJq2bANCMRd4=","title":"**What is wrong.** `web/src/routes/alerts.tsx` holds the keyset cursor and the current filters as two separate signals, reset in two separate `createEffect`s (`:99-109` for the filter fingerprint, `:111-118` for the roll-up axis). Both effects exist to keep a cursor from outliving the filters it was minted under — the file's own comment at `:94-97` says why: *\"Any filter change invalidates every cursor minted under the old filters (§E.3 answers a stale one with `400 cursor_filter_mismatch`), so the stack resets.\"* But `createEffect` runs too late to prevent that request from being built. solid-query's observer reads the query key from a `createComputed` — `web/node_modules/@tanstack/solid-query/src/useBaseQuery.ts:361-370`:","message":"```\ncreateComputed(\n  on(\n    [observer, defaultedOptions],\n    ([obs, opts]) =\u003e {\n      obs.setOptions(opts)\n      setStateWithReconciliation(obs.getOptimisticResult(opts))\n      refetch()\n    },\n    { defer: true },\n  ),\n)\n```\n\n`createComputed` is Solid's *pure* phase, which runs before `createEffect`. So when a filter changes: `filters()` → `filterFingerprint()` → `compiled()` (`:120`, reading `cursor()`) all recompute synchronously in the pure phase, and the `createComputed` above fires `setOptions`+`refetch` with the NEW filter still paired to the OLD cursor, before the `createEffect` at `:99` ever runs to call `setCursor(null)`. The same race applies to the roll-up query via the axis effect at `:111-118` and `compileRollupFilters` at `:135-137`.\n\n**The demonstration.** `qk.alerts.list` (`web/src/api/keys.ts:22`) is `[\"alerts\", \"list\", query] as const` — the compiled query object, cursor included, IS the cache key, so a (new filter, old cursor) pairing is a distinct key that gets its own fetch, not a no-op. The server binds a cursor to the filter set that minted it: `internal/alerts/service/read.go:68-70` — `if !q.Page.Cursor.IsZero() \u0026\u0026 q.Page.Cursor.Hash != q.Filter.FilterHash { return ListResult{}, errs.Malformed(\"cursor_filter_mismatch\", ...) }` — and the decode-time twin in `internal/platform/httpx/cursor.go:77-79`. `docs/design/SPEC.md:1795` states the contract directly: *\"A cursor whose `h` does not match the current filter set is rejected `400 cursor_filter_mismatch`.\"* So every filter or `group_by` change on this route sends one request the server is contractually obligated to refuse.\n\nThe identical hazard is named and guarded against, in the same wording, in `web/src/features/settings/RejectionsPanel.tsx:156-162` (added in commit `5dde27d`): *\"A CURSOR CANNOT OUTLIVE THE FILTER THAT MINTED IT. §E.3 answers a cursor carried across a filter change with `400 cursor_filter_mismatch`, and resetting it from an effect is too late to prevent that: solid-query reads the query key in Solid's pure phase, so a request pairing the old cursor with the new reasons is built and sent before any effect gets to run.\"* That file holds `{reasons, cursor, kept}` as one `FeedView` value (`:164-169`) and resets all three in the single synchronous `onChange` that changes the filter (`:261`), so the doomed pairing is never constructed. It is worth noting `git log` shows only that one commit touching `RejectionsPanel.tsx` — the correct pattern was built in from the start there, not retrofitted after a bug; `alerts.tsx` is the one place that still has the two-effect shape the comment warns against.\n\n**Why it matters — the user-visible consequence, stated plainly.** This is a wasted-request bug, not (in the ordinary render path) a visible one. Solid runs every pure computation for a signal update — including the `createComputed` that dispatches the wrong request — synchronously and fully before any `createEffect` runs, and both happen before the browser gets a chance to paint. So the corrective `setCursor(null)`/`setBucketCursor(null)` effect also fires, and the observer switches to the corrected query key, before the erroneous request's response can arrive or render. In the common case there is no flash of `ErrorState`, no lost page, and no retry storm the user sees — the failed fetch becomes an orphaned, unobserved query-cache entry. It is real waste, though: every filter and `group_by` change sends one guaranteed-400 to the server, and the global `retry: 1` in `web/src/App.tsx:28` means TanStack retries that dead request once more before giving up on it. `web/src/components/ui/states.tsx:130-131` already carries a friendly message for `cursor_filter_mismatch` (\"The page cursor was minted under different filters. Pagination has been reset.\"), which shows the team anticipated this error class reaching the UI in some path — but under this route's own render order, this particular race is not that path.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/routes/alerts.tsx:94-118` (the two effects and the comment describing the hazard they don't actually prevent), `:120` and `:135-137` (`compiled`/`rollupCompiled`, the memos the query keys read), `:122-133` and `:139-145` (the two `useQuery` calls). Precedent: `web/src/features/settings/RejectionsPanel.tsx:156-262` (commit `5dde27d`). The pure-phase mechanism: `web/node_modules/@tanstack/solid-query/src/useBaseQuery.ts:361-370`. The server-side binding: `internal/alerts/service/read.go:58-70`, `internal/platform/httpx/cursor.go:56-81`, `docs/design/SPEC.md:1795`. Retry default: `web/src/App.tsx:23-33`. The already-anticipated error copy: `web/src/components/ui/states.tsx:130-131`.\n\n**Scope.** small — one file, one state shape to consolidate (`cursor`, `kept`, `pageCount` for the list; `bucketCursor`, `keptBuckets` for the roll-up), following the exact `FeedView` pattern already proven in `RejectionsPanel.tsx`.\n\n**Done when.** `web/src/routes/alerts.tsx` holds cursor/kept state for the list and for the roll-up as single values updated synchronously alongside the filter or axis change that invalidates them (mirroring `RejectionsPanel.tsx`'s `FeedView`), so no `createEffect` is relied on to reset a cursor after a query has already been constructed against it, and a filter or `group_by` change never issues a request carrying a cursor from the filters it was minted under.","files":null}]}